WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.270
Video Streaming and Live Cricket.

2
00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:07.457
Chapter I06, from the Industry Designs part of the System Design Playbook.

3
00:00:09.207 --> 00:00:10.167
At a glance:

4
00:00:10.447 --> 00:00:11.620
Chapter at a glance.

5
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:12.711
Sector:

6
00:00:12.991 --> 00:00:18.303
Media and entertainment, Format: system design walkthrough (no code), Reader bands:

7
00:00:18.583 --> 00:00:20.631
B1, then B4, Builds on:

8
00:00:20.911 --> 00:00:27.780
Ch 52 (object storage, CDN), Ch 25, Ch 43, Ch 51.

9
00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:46.855
What you get: the design of a streaming platform that does two very different things: video on demand (VOD): films and series, Netflix-style;. live sport at extreme concurrency: a cricket final watched by tens of millions of people at the same moment, Hotstar or JioCinema-style.

10
00:00:47.135 --> 00:00:48.031
It covers:

11
00:00:49.381 --> 00:01:07.557
the VOD pipeline: ingest, per-title encoding, quality measurement, packaging, DRM, CDN placement, playback, adaptive bitrate;. the live pipeline: camera to phone in seconds, redundant encoders, low-latency packaging, multi-CDN, ads inserted into the stream,

12
00:01:07.837 --> 00:01:28.978
and the everyone joins at the toss problem;. the product around the video: catalogue, personalisation (including artwork), search, subscriptions and entitlements, ads, downloads, reactions and live scores;. the algorithms: bitrate ladders, VMAF, ABR, CDN steering and cache fill, recommendations, concurrency forecasting, ad decisioning;.

13
00:01:30.328 --> 00:01:44.941
the data platform: playback telemetry at millions of events per second, real-time quality-of-experience dashboards, the lakehouse, experimentation;. the tech stack: what Netflix and Hotstar have published, and a reference choice.

14
00:01:45.221 --> 00:01:46.203
Reading time:

15
