00:04:55.555 --> 00:04:57.709
Scale for this design (assumptions).

84
00:04:57.989 --> 00:04:59.333
Here is a quick table:

85
00:04:59.613 --> 00:05:00.936
Quantity, and Value.

86
00:05:01.216 --> 00:05:03.563
Subscribers or monthly active users:

87
00:05:03.843 --> 00:05:07.043
60M or 150M (with a free tier).

88
00:05:07.323 --> 00:05:08.560
VOD catalogue:

89
00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:12.680
100k titles, ~1M hours after episodes.

90
00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:14.773
VOD peak concurrent streams:

91
00:05:15.053 --> 00:05:15.885
8M.

92
00:05:16.165 --> 00:05:18.064
Live final peak concurrency:

93
00:05:18.344 --> 00:05:26.536
40M+ concurrent viewers (records reported for Indian cricket streams have been in the tens of millions; VERIFY current records).

94
00:05:26.816 --> 00:05:32.171
Average live bitrate delivered: ~1.5 Mbps (mostly mobile).

95
00:05:33.521 --> 00:05:36.166
Estimation, why live cricket is extreme:

96
00:05:36.446 --> 00:05:37.299
Egress:

97
00:05:37.579 --> 00:05:43.318
40M times 1.5 Mbps about 60 Tbps of delivery at the peak.

98
00:05:43.598 --> 00:05:47.843
That only works with multiple CDNs and ISP-embedded caches.

99
00:05:48.123 --> 00:05:56.721
Segment requests: with 4-second segments, each viewer requests a new video segment every 4 s, plus audio and manifest refreshes.

100
00:05:57.001 --> 00:06:02.675
That is roughly 20 to 30M HTTP requests per second at the CDN edges.

101
00:06:02.955 --> 00:06:06.987
Origin load: the origin must see only a tiny fraction of that.

102
00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:11.833
Shielding and request coalescing are mandatory (§5.6).

103
00:06:13.183 --> 00:06:14.121
Telemetry:

104
00:06:14.401 --> 00:06:22.273
40M players sending a heartbeat every 30 s is ~1.3M events per second, more with per-segment events.

105
00:06:22.553 --> 00:06:29.337
Joins: if 20M people join within 10 minutes of the toss, that is ~33k joins per second.

106
00:06:29.617 --> 00:06:35.761
Each join means login or token checks, entitlement, playback session creation and a manifest.

107
00:06:36.041 --> 00:06:39.732
The API tier, not the video, is usually what breaks first.

108
00:06:41.082 --> 00:06:41.957
Part 2.

109
00:06:42.237 --> 00:06:43.197
Requirements.

110
00:06:44.447 --> 00:06:45.300
Functional.

111
00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:54.561
Browse, search and get recommendations; watch VOD with resume across devices; download for offline viewing (within licence rules).

112
00:06:54.841 --> 00:07:03.695
Watch live matches with multiple feeds: languages, camera angles, commentary; with DVR (pause, rewind within a window) and go live.

113
00:07:03.975 --> 00:07:13.596
Subscriptions and free tiers with ads; plans by device count and quality; payments including UPI autopay mandates (VERIFY mandate rules).

114
00:07:13.876 --> 00:07:16.735
Profiles, parental controls, content ratings.

115
00:07:17.015 --> 00:07:23.863
Ads: pre-roll for VOD on ad tiers; ad breaks in live matches (between overs), targeted per viewer.

116
00:07:25.213 --> 00:07:29.629
Live companion features: scorecard, key moments, reactions, polls.

117
00:07:29.909 --> 00:07:34.453
Rights enforcement: territories, windows, device types, concurrent streams.

118
00:07:35.803 --> 00:07:36.912
Non-functional.

119
00:07:37.192 --> 00:07:38.536
Here is a quick table:

120
00:07:38.816 --> 00:07:40.181
Attribute, and Target.

121
00:07:40.461 --> 00:07:41.421
Start time:

122
00:07:41.701 --> 00:07:47.205
Video starts in 2 s (p50) and 5 s (p95) on mobile networks.

123
00:07:47.485 --> 00:07:48.381
Rebuffering:

124
00:07:48.661 --> 00:07:52.139
Rebuffer ratio 0.5% of watch time.

125
00:07:52.419 --> 00:07:53.571
Live latency:

126
00:07:53.851 --> 00:08:00.827
Glass-to-glass 10 to 30 s standard, 3 to 8 s low-latency mode (TV broadcast is also delayed).

127
00:08:01.107 --> 00:08:02.173
Availability:

128
00:08:02.453 --> 00:08:10.987
Live playback ≥ 99.99% during matches; no single point of failure from camera to CDN.

129
00:08:11.267 --> 00:08:12.013
Scale:

130
00:08:12.293 --> 00:08:17.328
Absorb 40M concurrent viewers and joins of tens of thousands per second.

131
00:08:17.608 --> 00:08:18.611
Security:

132
00:08:18.891 --> 00:08:25.781
DRM for premium content; token-authenticated URLs; forensic watermarking for high-value live.

133
00:08:26.061 --> 00:08:27.064
Efficiency:

134
00:08:27.344 --> 00:08:31.376
Minimise bits per quality (encoding) and origin egress (caching).

135
00:08:32.726 --> 00:08:33.622
Part 3.

136
00:08:33.902 --> 00:08:35.417
High-Level Architecture.

137
00:08:36.667 --> 00:08:38.011
Here is the diagram.

138
00:08:41.861 --> 00:08:42.821
Two planes:

139
00:08:43.101 --> 00:08:51.762
The data plane (video bytes: encoders, then packager, then origin, then CDN, then player) must be simple, redundant and cacheable.

140
00:08:52.042 --> 00:08:59.445
The control plane (who can watch what, from where, at what quality, with which ads) is where most application logic lives.

141
00:08:59.725 --> 00:09:04.546
At join time it must scale like an e-commerce flash sale (Ch 46).

142
00:09:05.896 --> 00:09:06.813
Part 4.

143
00:09:07.093 --> 00:09:08.971
How It Works, Journeys End to End.

144
00:09:10.221 --> 00:09:13.698
Journey 1: a new series goes from master file to playable.

145
00:09:13.978 --> 00:09:22.362
Ingest: the studio delivers a mezzanine master: very high bitrate video, multichannel audio, subtitle files and artwork.

146
00:09:22.642 --> 00:09:28.039
Automated checks: format, corrupt frames, audio sync, loudness, subtitle timing.

147
00:09:28.319 --> 00:09:30.175
Failures return to the studio.

148
00:09:30.455 --> 00:09:34.807
Analysis: the video is split into shots, using scene-change detection.

149
00:09:35.087 --> 00:09:42.170
Complexity is measured per shot, because a dark, static dialogue scene needs far fewer bits than a stadium crowd.

150
00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:43.303
Encoding:

151
00:09:44.653 --> 00:10:02.659
each shot is encoded at many resolution times bitrate (or quality) points, in parallel on a large compute farm;. the per-title or shot-based optimisation picks the most efficient combinations (§6.1);. multiple codecs are produced for different devices:

152
00:10:02.939 --> 00:10:11.045
H.264 for compatibility, HEVC or VP9 or AV1 for efficiency on supporting devices.

153
00:10:11.325 --> 00:10:21.011
Netflix has described per-title encoding (2015) and shot-based dynamic optimisation (2018) publicly (VERIFY).

154
00:10:22.361 --> 00:10:34.051
Quality measurement: every encode is scored with VMAF, a perceptual quality metric Netflix open-sourced (VERIFY), so the ladder is chosen by perceived quality, not bitrate alone.

155
00:10:34.331 --> 00:10:40.753
Packaging: encodes are cut into segments of a few seconds each, in CMAF (fragmented MP4);.

156
00:10:41.033 --> 00:10:48.030
HLS and DASH manifests list the renditions;. audio tracks per language, and subtitles as separate tracks.

157
00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:55.908
DRM: segments are encrypted (common encryption) with content keys stored in a key management service.

158
00:10:56.188 --> 00:11:00.753
Licences are issued at playback by the right DRM system per device:

159
00:11:01.033 --> 00:11:05.300
Widevine (Android, Chrome), FairPlay (Apple), PlayReady (Windows, TVs).

160
00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:19.703
Publishing: packaged files go to the origin (object storage);. the catalogue record goes live when the rights window opens, per territory;. artwork variants are generated for personalisation (§6.6).

161
00:11:21.053 --> 00:11:33.789
Pre-positioning: popularity is predicted per region;. popular titles are filled into ISP-embedded caches overnight, during off-peak hours, so peak-time viewing rarely touches the backbone;.

162
00:11:34.069 --> 00:11:39.999
Netflix's Open Connect appliances in ISP networks are the public example (VERIFY).

163
00:11:41.349 --> 00:11:43.504
Journey 2: pressing Play on VOD.

164
00:11:43.784 --> 00:11:52.765
Request: the app calls the playback service with the title, device capabilities (codecs, DRM, resolution, HDR) and network hints.

165
00:11:53.045 --> 00:12:05.163
Checks: entitlement: is the subscription active, and does the plan allow this quality and the number of concurrent streams? rights: is the title available in this territory now? device limits.

166
00:12:05.443 --> 00:12:07.064
The playback service returns:

167
00:12:08.414 --> 00:12:16.606
a manifest, tailored to the device: only playable codecs and resolutions, possibly personalised to start at a sensible bitrate;.

168
00:12:16.886 --> 00:12:22.795
CDN URLs: the best cache for this client, chosen by steering (§6.4).

169
00:12:23.075 --> 00:12:29.006
The URLs are token-authenticated and expire;. a licence request token for DRM.

170
00:12:29.286 --> 00:12:37.201
Licence: the player requests a DRM licence from the licence service, which checks the token and policy (offline allowed?

171
00:12:37.481 --> 00:12:39.827
HDCP required for 4K?).

172
00:12:40.107 --> 00:12:49.985
Buffering: the player fetches the first segments at a conservative bitrate, starts playback once a small buffer is built, then adapts bitrate (§6.3).

173
00:12:51.335 --> 00:12:57.649
Resume: the playback position is saved every few seconds, so another device resumes from the same point.

174
00:12:57.929 --> 00:13:06.655
Telemetry: start time, bitrate switches, rebuffer events, errors and CDN used are sent to the telemetry pipeline (§7).

175
00:13:08.005 --> 00:13:12.271
Journey 3: a cricket final, from the stadium to 40 million phones.

176
00:13:12.551 --> 00:13:24.839
Production: the broadcaster's production (tens of cameras, graphics, commentary) outputs program feeds: a world feed plus language commentary feeds, and sometimes extra camera angles.

177
00:13:25.119 --> 00:13:41.077
Contribution: feeds are encoded at high quality and sent over two independent paths (fibre and satellite, or two ISPs) to two cloud regions;. reliable streaming protocols (SRT, RIST, or dedicated links) survive packet loss.

178
00:13:41.357 --> 00:13:43.618
The 1 plus1 redundancy starts here.

179
00:13:44.968 --> 00:13:59.752
Live transcoding: in each region, live transcoders produce the ABR ladder, for example, 240p at 200 kbps up to 1080p at 6 Mbps, plus audio per language.

180
00:14:00.032 --> 00:14:03.509
Encoding runs with a latency budget of a second or two.

181
00:14:03.789 --> 00:14:09.848
Transcoders run in pairs, time-aligned, so the packager can switch between them without a visible glitch.

182
00:14:10.128 --> 00:14:24.933
Packaging: the live packager produces CMAF segments (for example, 4 s, or 2 s with partial segments for low latency), updates manifests every segment, encrypts with DRM if required, and writes to the origin.

183
00:14:25.213 --> 00:14:29.757
A DVR window (for example, the whole match) stays available for rewind.

184
00:14:31.107 --> 00:14:33.006
Origin shield and CDNs:

185
00:14:33.286 --> 00:14:44.913
CDNs fetch from an origin shield layer, a tier of caches in front of the origin, not from the origin directly;. every segment is requested from the origin once per shield node.

186
00:14:45.193 --> 00:14:57.438
Millions of viewer requests collapse into a handful of origin fetches through request coalescing (§5.6);. multiple CDNs and ISP-embedded caches share the load.

187
00:14:57.718 --> 00:15:00.897
Joining: millions of viewers open the app before the toss.

188
00:15:01.177 --> 00:15:02.371
The join path is:

189
00:15:03.721 --> 00:15:13.940
home page (cached, mostly static);. match page;. playback session: entitlement, device check, ad profile, CDN assignment;. manifest;. segments.

190
00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:17.761
The control plane is pre-scaled for the expected join rate.

191
00:15:18.041 --> 00:15:21.433
Non-essential calls are deferred (§5.7).

192
00:15:22.783 --> 00:15:25.983
Watching: the player fetches segments and adapts bitrate.

193
00:15:26.263 --> 00:15:31.767
On congested mobile networks it may settle at 360p, 480p.

194
00:15:32.047 --> 00:15:34.309
Telemetry flows every few seconds.

195
00:15:34.589 --> 00:15:52.039
Ad breaks between overs: the production signals an ad break with SCTE-35 markers in the stream;. the server-side ad insertion (SSAI) service asks ad decisioning which ads to show each viewer or viewer segment, then splices ad segments into the manifest;.

196
00:15:53.389 --> 00:16:00.024
viewers see personalised ads seamlessly, and ad blockers can't easily strip them (§5.8).

197
00:16:00.304 --> 00:16:04.059
Spikes: at a wicket, social media sends a wave of new joins.

198
00:16:04.339 --> 00:16:06.408
At the last over, concurrency peaks.

199
00:16:06.688 --> 00:16:12.256
Real-time QoE dashboards watch rebuffer ratio per CDN, ISP and region.

200
00:16:12.536 --> 00:16:16.461
Steering shifts new sessions away from a struggling CDN within a minute.

201
00:16:17.811 --> 00:16:20.265
After the match: viewers leave within minutes.

202
00:16:20.545 --> 00:16:26.603
Highlights (auto-clipped key moments, §5.9) are published as VOD within minutes.

203
00:16:26.883 --> 00:16:28.462
Capacity is scaled down.

204
00:16:29.812 --> 00:16:32.777
Journey 4: subscribing with UPI autopay.

205
00:16:33.057 --> 00:16:37.089
Plan choice: the user picks a plan (mobile-only, super, premium).

206
00:16:37.369 --> 00:16:49.572
Mandate: the payment service creates a recurring mandate (UPI AutoPay or card e-mandate), subject to RBI rules on recurring payments and pre-debit notifications (VERIFY).

207
00:16:49.852 --> 00:16:59.388
Entitlement: on success, the entitlements service grants the plan's rights: device count, max resolution, ad-free or not, live sports access.

208
00:16:59.668 --> 00:17:02.207
The rights are cached at the edge for playback checks.

209
00:17:03.557 --> 00:17:07.802
Renewals run on a schedule, with pre-debit notifications as required.

210
00:17:08.082 --> 00:17:15.762
Failed renewals get retries and a grace period before downgrade, so dunning never cuts a viewer off mid-match (a policy choice).

211
00:17:16.042 --> 00:17:21.119
Password sharing: concurrent stream limits are enforced per account by a session counter.

212
00:17:21.399 --> 00:17:26.434
Sharing detection uses devices and locations (VERIFY policies per market).

213
00:17:27.784 --> 00:17:30.963
Journey 5: downloading an episode for a train journey.

214
00:17:31.243 --> 00:17:34.869
Request: the user downloads an episode at a chosen quality.

215
00:17:35.149 --> 00:17:41.869
The licence service issues an offline licence with an expiry, and download count limits per the content licence.

216
00:17:42.149 --> 00:17:46.288
Download: the device downloads encrypted segments for one rendition.

217
00:17:46.568 --> 00:17:49.597
Playback works offline until the licence expires.

218
00:17:49.877 --> 00:17:52.693
The licence renews when the device is online.

219
00:17:54.043 --> 00:17:55.003
Part 5.

220
00:17:55.283 --> 00:17:56.819
Core Subsystems in Depth.

221
00:17:58.069 --> 00:17:59.840
The VOD encoding pipeline.

222
00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:17.485
A workflow of many small tasks: split into chunks or shots;. encode each chunk times each ladder point times each codec, in parallel on thousands of machines (spot capacity is fine, because a lost task just retries);. measure quality;. assemble;. package.

223
00:18:18.835 --> 00:18:24.489
It is orchestrated by a workflow engine with retries and idempotent tasks (Ch 50).

224
00:18:24.769 --> 00:18:29.206
Netflix has described its media pipeline platform publicly (VERIFY).

225
00:18:29.486 --> 00:18:30.467
Economics:

226
00:18:30.747 --> 00:18:38.513
AV1 or HEVC encoding costs far more compute than H.264, but saves bandwidth on every view.

227
00:18:38.793 --> 00:18:44.361
So it pays for popular titles first, and the codec set per title depends on predicted views.

228
00:18:44.641 --> 00:18:52.449
Re-encoding: when better encoders arrive, the back catalogue is re-encoded in priority order of expected bandwidth savings.

229
00:18:53.799 --> 00:18:56.081
Packaging, manifests and DRM.

230
00:18:56.361 --> 00:19:02.484
CMAF lets HLS and DASH share the same media segments, which halves storage and cache footprint.

231
00:19:02.764 --> 00:19:13.580
Manifests are generated per request (or per device class) from templates, so they can: filter renditions by device;. add CDN tokens;. insert ads (SSAI).

232
00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:17.956
Manifest generation must be very fast and cacheable per class.

233
00:19:19.306 --> 00:19:39.850
DRM: one set of encrypted segments (CENC; CBCS mode covers all modern DRMs);. licence servers per DRM system;. keys in an HSM-backed key service;. key rotation for live;. licence policies (HDCP, offline, security level) enforce studio and sports-rights requirements.

234
00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:42.331
Playback service.

235
00:19:42.611 --> 00:19:46.877
One decision point for may this viewer watch this now, how, and from where.

236
00:19:47.157 --> 00:19:57.739
It combines: entitlements and rights (territory, window);. device capabilities;. concurrent-stream counts;. experiment assignments (for example, a new ABR);.

237
00:19:58.019 --> 00:20:00.707
CDN steering (§6.4).

238
00:20:02.057 --> 00:20:05.513
It is the most critical control-plane service during live events.

239
00:20:05.793 --> 00:20:14.113
Precompute and cache what can be cached, for example, rights per title per territory, and keep the per-request work to token signing and steering.

240
00:20:15.463 --> 00:20:16.892
CDN strategy.

241
00:20:17.172 --> 00:20:18.516
Here is a quick table:

242
00:20:18.796 --> 00:20:20.012
Option, and Role.

243
00:20:20.292 --> 00:20:24.239
ISP-embedded caches (own appliances inside ISPs):

244
00:20:24.519 --> 00:20:30.001
Serve most VOD peak traffic locally; filled off-peak; cheapest per GB at scale.

245
00:20:30.281 --> 00:20:32.073
Commercial CDNs (several):

246
00:20:32.353 --> 00:20:36.044
Burst capacity for live, geographic coverage, redundancy.

247
00:20:36.324 --> 00:20:37.497
Origin shield:

248
00:20:37.777 --> 00:20:41.873
Collapses cache misses from many edges into few origin fetches.

249
00:20:42.153 --> 00:20:43.327
Steering service:

250
00:20:43.607 --> 00:20:49.431
Chooses a CDN per session (and can switch mid-session) based on real-time QoE and cost.

251
00:20:50.781 --> 00:20:53.213
VOD: the cache holds a popular subset.

252
00:20:53.493 --> 00:20:59.594
Fill is planned: which titles and renditions go to which cache, overnight, from popularity forecasts.

253
00:20:59.874 --> 00:21:15.789
Live: every edge needs the same newest segment at the same time;. edges must coalesce concurrent misses into one upstream request;. manifests have short TTLs ( about the segment duration);. segments have long TTLs once published.

254
00:21:17.139 --> 00:21:18.611
The player and ABR.

255
00:21:18.891 --> 00:21:30.475
The player's control loop: estimate bandwidth;. track buffer level;. choose the next segment's rendition;. handle errors: retry on another CDN URL, skip a bad segment.

256
00:21:30.755 --> 00:21:38.307
The ABR algorithm (§6.3) balances three goals: high quality, no rebuffering, and few switches.

257
00:21:39.657 --> 00:21:51.881
Live players also manage latency: they target a buffer that keeps them close to the live edge, speeding playback slightly (for example, 1.05 times ) to catch up after stalls.

258
00:21:52.161 --> 00:21:58.113
Telemetry: the player reports every session's key events, which feeds QoE analytics and steering.

259
00:21:59.463 --> 00:22:01.937
Handling live concurrency, the data plane.

260
00:22:02.217 --> 00:22:16.041
Segment request coalescing: when 100k viewers on one edge ask for segment 1832 at the same second, the edge sends one request upstream and serves all waiters from the result.

261
00:22:16.321 --> 00:22:21.569
The shield does the same towards the origin (Ch 07 stampede protection).

262
00:22:21.849 --> 00:22:25.519
Manifest caching: live manifests change every segment.

263
00:22:25.799 --> 00:22:32.049
Cache them for about half a segment duration at the edge, so manifest requests don't all hit the origin.

264
00:22:32.329 --> 00:22:33.759
Capacity reservation:

265
00:22:35.109 --> 00:22:41.381
CDN capacity is reserved per match, per region, weeks in advance, from concurrency forecasts;.

266
00:22:41.661 --> 00:22:45.010
ISP caches are pre-warmed;. peering links are checked.

267
00:22:45.290 --> 00:22:54.997
Bitrate capping under stress: if total demand approaches delivery capacity, the steering service caps the maximum bitrate for new sessions or for mobile.

268
00:22:55.277 --> 00:22:58.775
A little less quality for everyone beats rebuffering for many.

269
00:23:00.125 --> 00:23:02.749
Handling live concurrency, the control plane.

270
00:23:03.029 --> 00:23:09.216
Public talks from Hotstar engineers on IPL-scale traffic describe these patterns (VERIFY):

271
00:23:09.496 --> 00:23:11.971
Pre-scaling, not reactive autoscaling.

272
00:23:12.251 --> 00:23:20.549
Capacity is scaled ahead of the toss from concurrency forecasts, because autoscaling can't add capacity fast enough for the join spike.

273
00:23:20.829 --> 00:23:33.523
Degradation switches (panic mode): under extreme load, turn off non-essential features, such as: personalised recommendations (serve a static popular row);. watch history writes (batch them later);.

274
00:23:34.873 --> 00:23:37.731
some API calls (serve cached responses).

275
00:23:38.011 --> 00:23:40.123
This protects the join and playback path.

276
00:23:40.403 --> 00:23:46.526
Static, cacheable responses for the match page and home page during the event, served from the CDN.

277
00:23:46.806 --> 00:23:55.489
Authentication: token validation at the edge without calling the identity service per request; long-lived tokens refreshed lazily.

278
00:23:55.769 --> 00:24:01.870
Retry discipline in clients: exponential backoff with jitter, and server-provided retry-after.

279
00:24:02.150 --> 00:24:07.526
A synchronised retry storm from 30M apps can take down anything (Ch 18).

280
00:24:08.876 --> 00:24:14.892
Load testing at the expected peak with realistic join curves before the season, and game days.

281
00:24:16.242 --> 00:24:18.119
Ads: server-side insertion.

282
00:24:18.399 --> 00:24:19.231
Signals:

283
00:24:19.511 --> 00:24:24.802
SCTE-35 markers in the live stream mark ad break start and duration.

284
00:24:25.082 --> 00:24:26.127
SSAI flow:

285
00:24:26.407 --> 00:24:35.602
When a viewer's manifest reaches a break, the SSAI service calls ad decisioning with the viewer's profile (segment, location, device, frequency caps).

286
00:24:35.882 --> 00:24:38.634
It receives ads (standard VAST responses).

287
00:24:38.914 --> 00:24:41.943
It stitches ad segments into that viewer's manifest.

288
00:24:43.293 --> 00:24:47.624
Ads are pre-transcoded to match the content ladder, so switching is seamless.

289
00:24:47.904 --> 00:25:03.200
Scale trick: personalising every manifest for 40M viewers at the same break is expensive;. so viewers are grouped into cohorts (hundreds to thousands of cohorts), each with a personalised ad pod;. manifests are then cached per cohort.

290
00:25:04.550 --> 00:25:19.419
Ad decisioning: campaigns with targeting, budgets and pacing;. frequency caps (the same ad not more than N times per match);. competitive separation (no two competing brands back-to-back);. an auction for programmatic demand.

291
00:25:19.699 --> 00:25:25.459
Measurement: impression beacons from the player, or server-side beacons, reconciled for billing.

292
00:25:25.739 --> 00:25:27.681
Viewability and completion rates.

293
00:25:29.031 --> 00:25:30.609
Live experience features.

294
00:25:30.889 --> 00:25:36.415
Scorecard and ball-by-ball data come from a sports data provider or official scoring feed.

295
00:25:36.695 --> 00:25:46.551
A low-latency pub or sub fan-out pushes updates to apps over persistent connections, or apps poll a CDN-cached JSON with a short TTL.

296
00:25:46.831 --> 00:25:49.689
The cached approach scales trivially to tens of millions.

297
00:25:49.969 --> 00:26:01.383
Key moments: wickets, sixes and milestones from the scoring feed trigger auto-clipping (cut the live DVR around the moment, package it as a clip) for highlights and notifications.

298
00:26:02.733 --> 00:26:20.034
Reactions and polls: millions of emoji taps per second;. aggregate them at the edge or ingestion tier into counts per second per emoji;. broadcast only aggregated counts back;. never store every tap individually for real-time display (§6.8).

299
00:26:20.314 --> 00:26:27.631
Multi-language commentary as separate audio tracks or separate streams, with language chosen at the start and switchable.

300
00:26:28.981 --> 00:26:31.328
Catalogue, personalisation and search.

301
00:26:31.608 --> 00:26:40.355
Catalogue: titles, seasons and episodes, cast, genres, languages, maturity ratings, and rights windows per territory and platform.

302
00:26:40.635 --> 00:26:45.051
Its change events feed search indexes and the playback service's rights cache.

303
00:26:45.331 --> 00:26:48.851
Home page: rows (Continue watching, Because you watched...

304
00:26:49.131 --> 00:26:53.269
Top 10 in India today, Live now) chosen and ordered per profile.

305
00:26:53.549 --> 00:26:57.219
Row selection and ranking are learned (§6.6).

306
00:26:57.499 --> 00:27:03.216
Artwork per title is personalised: the same film shown with a different image to different viewers.

307
00:27:03.496 --> 00:27:05.779
Netflix has written about this (VERIFY).

308
00:27:07.129 --> 00:27:18.670
Search: titles, people and genres, with multilingual and transliterated queries (Ch 22, Ch 42), and not available handling for titles outside the catalogue.

309
00:27:20.020 --> 00:27:21.961
Content protection and piracy.

310
00:27:22.241 --> 00:27:25.121
DRM for encryption and licence policies.

311
00:27:25.401 --> 00:27:32.228
Token-authenticated URLs with short expiry and binding to session or IP ranges where feasible.

312
00:27:32.508 --> 00:27:41.084
Forensic watermarking for live sport: invisible per-session marks let the platform identify the leaking account of a pirated restream.

313
00:27:41.364 --> 00:27:48.617
It is implemented by A or B segment variants: each viewer's manifest selects a unique sequence of variant segments.

314
00:27:48.897 --> 00:27:51.927
Monitoring of piracy sites, and takedown workflows.

315
00:27:53.277 --> 00:27:54.215
Part 6.

316
00:27:54.495 --> 00:27:55.455
Algorithms.

317
00:27:56.705 --> 00:27:58.561
Encoding ladder optimisation.

318
00:27:58.841 --> 00:28:07.439
The problem: choose the set of (resolution, bitrate) renditions that give the best quality for each bandwidth level, per title or per shot.

319
00:28:07.719 --> 00:28:08.465
Method:

320
00:28:08.745 --> 00:28:12.137
Encode sample points across resolutions and bitrates.

321
00:28:12.417 --> 00:28:16.087
Measure quality (VMAF) versus bitrate for each resolution.

322
00:28:16.367 --> 00:28:22.639
The convex hull of the quality, bitrate points across all resolutions gives the most efficient choices.

323
00:28:22.919 --> 00:28:26.801
Pick ladder rungs along the hull at target bitrate or quality steps.

324
00:28:28.151 --> 00:28:31.906
Easy content (animation) reaches high quality at low bitrate.

325
00:28:32.186 --> 00:28:34.938
Complex content (sport, grain) needs more.

326
00:28:35.218 --> 00:28:48.829
Shot-based optimisation: choose encoding parameters per shot along the hull to minimise total bits for a target quality, or to maximise quality for a bit budget (a Lagrangian-style trade-off across shots).

327
00:28:50.179 --> 00:28:51.971
Perceptual quality (VMAF).

328
00:28:52.251 --> 00:28:56.752
Why not PSNR or SSIM: they correlate poorly with what viewers perceive.

329
00:28:57.032 --> 00:29:09.171
VMAF fuses several elementary metrics (visual information fidelity, detail loss, motion) with a machine-learned model trained on human ratings, producing a 0 to 100 score.

330
00:29:09.451 --> 00:29:15.125
Uses: ladder selection, encoder comparisons, and regression tests for encoder changes.

331
00:29:16.475 --> 00:29:18.395
Adaptive bitrate (ABR).

332
00:29:18.675 --> 00:29:20.019
Here is a quick table:

333
00:29:20.299 --> 00:29:22.305
Family, and Idea, and Weakness.

334
00:29:22.585 --> 00:29:23.673
Throughput-based.

335
00:29:23.953 --> 00:29:24.742
Idea:

336
00:29:25.022 --> 00:29:29.545
Pick the highest bitrate below the estimated bandwidth times safety factor.

337
00:29:29.825 --> 00:29:30.678
Weakness:

338
00:29:30.958 --> 00:29:33.433
Noisy estimates on mobile; oscillation.

339
00:29:33.713 --> 00:29:35.313
Buffer-based (BBA).

340
00:29:35.593 --> 00:29:36.382
Idea:

341
00:29:36.662 --> 00:29:41.718
Map buffer level to bitrate: low buffer, then low bitrate, high buffer, then high.

342
00:29:41.998 --> 00:29:42.851
Weakness:

343
00:29:43.131 --> 00:29:45.435
Slow start; ignores throughput info.

344
00:29:45.715 --> 00:29:46.462
BOLA.

345
00:29:46.742 --> 00:29:47.531
Idea:

346
00:29:47.811 --> 00:29:54.041
Lyapunov optimisation on buffer, maximising a utility of quality minus rebuffer risk.

347
00:29:54.321 --> 00:29:55.174
Weakness:

348
00:29:55.454 --> 00:29:57.011
Tuning for live latency.

349
00:29:57.291 --> 00:29:59.275
Model predictive (MPC).

350
00:29:59.555 --> 00:30:00.345
Idea:

351
00:30:00.625 --> 00:30:08.475
Predict throughput over the next few segments, choose the plan maximising quality minus rebuffer penalty minus switch penalty.

352
00:30:08.755 --> 00:30:09.609
Weakness:

353
00:30:09.889 --> 00:30:12.022
More compute; depends on predictions.

354
00:30:12.302 --> 00:30:13.753
Learned (RL-based).

355
00:30:14.033 --> 00:30:14.822
Idea:

356
00:30:15.102 --> 00:30:17.171
Train a policy on network traces.

357
00:30:17.451 --> 00:30:18.305
Weakness:

358
00:30:18.585 --> 00:30:20.782
Generalisation, explainability.

359
00:30:22.132 --> 00:30:27.935
Practice: a hybrid, with throughput estimates for the start and buffer-based control in steady state.

360
00:30:28.215 --> 00:30:36.620
It is tuned per device class and network type through A or B tests, measuring start time, rebuffer ratio, average bitrate and switches.

361
00:30:37.970 --> 00:30:40.295
CDN steering and request routing.

362
00:30:40.575 --> 00:30:41.578
Mechanisms:

363
00:30:41.858 --> 00:30:43.586
DNS-based routing (coarse);.

364
00:30:43.866 --> 00:30:51.653
HTTP redirect or manifest URL choice by the playback service (per session);. client-side switching on errors.

365
00:30:51.933 --> 00:31:02.237
Decision: for each (ISP, region, device) group, maintain real-time QoE per CDN (rebuffer ratio, start time, error rate, throughput) from telemetry.

366
00:31:02.517 --> 00:31:05.589
Assign new sessions to the best CDN subject to:

367
00:31:06.939 --> 00:31:17.776
capacity commitments;. cost (committed volumes per CDN);. exploration: send a small share to each CDN to keep measurements fresh, a multi-armed bandit approach.

368
00:31:18.056 --> 00:31:19.165
Reaction time:

369
00:31:19.445 --> 00:31:25.355
QoE is aggregated per minute, and bad CDNs are drained from new sessions within a minute or two.

370
00:31:26.705 --> 00:31:28.134
Cache fill and eviction.

371
00:31:28.414 --> 00:31:35.198
VOD fill: predict per-title, per-region popularity for the next day (time series + release calendars).

372
00:31:35.478 --> 00:31:43.841
Solve a knapsack-like selection of title renditions to maximise the expected bytes served from cache, subject to cache capacity.

373
00:31:44.121 --> 00:31:44.995
Eviction:

374
00:31:45.275 --> 00:31:49.414
LRU is often worse than popularity-aware policies for video.

375
00:31:49.694 --> 00:31:59.827
Use frequency-aware policies (for example, LFU with aging, or admission filters such as TinyLFU) so one-off views don't evict popular content.

376
00:32:00.107 --> 00:32:07.894
Live: tiny working sets (the latest segments); correctness of TTLs and coalescing matters more than eviction policy.

377
00:32:09.244 --> 00:32:11.036
Recommendations and artwork.

378
00:32:11.316 --> 00:32:22.089
Candidate generation: collaborative filtering (matrix factorisation of viewing), content similarity (genre, cast, language embeddings), trending, continue watching.

379
00:32:22.369 --> 00:32:30.391
Ranking: gradient-boosted or neural models predicting the probability that a profile plays and keeps watching (not just clicks).

380
00:32:30.671 --> 00:32:33.487
Features: history, time, device, language.

381
00:32:33.767 --> 00:32:38.033
Page construction: choose rows and order them with diversity constraints.

382
00:32:38.313 --> 00:32:40.340
Row-level and page-level models.

383
00:32:41.690 --> 00:32:44.613
Artwork personalisation: a contextual bandit.

384
00:32:44.893 --> 00:32:49.245
For each title, choose among artwork variants using the profile context.

385
00:32:49.525 --> 00:32:54.453
Explore occasionally and learn from plays (VERIFY Netflix's published approach).

386
00:32:54.733 --> 00:33:00.621
Evaluation: offline metrics plus online A or B tests on retention and hours streamed.

387
00:33:01.971 --> 00:33:04.253
Concurrency forecasting for live events.

388
00:33:04.533 --> 00:33:14.347
Inputs: teams playing, stage of tournament, time slot, day of week, match closeness, star players, competing events, historical curves for similar matches.

389
00:33:14.627 --> 00:33:23.011
Output: peak concurrency and join-rate curves (joins per second around toss, innings break, last overs), with confidence intervals.

390
00:33:23.291 --> 00:33:24.144
Uses:

391
00:33:24.424 --> 00:33:30.696
CDN reservation, control-plane pre-scaling, ad inventory estimates, operations staffing.

392
00:33:30.976 --> 00:33:40.512
In-match updating: the forecast is re-estimated during the match as real concurrency arrives, for example, a close finish raises the expected peak.

393
00:33:41.862 --> 00:33:45.361
Real-time aggregation at scale (reactions, telemetry).

394
00:33:45.641 --> 00:33:54.729
Reactions: clients batch taps (for example, send once per second with counts), and ingestion nodes aggregate per emoji per second locally.

395
00:33:55.009 --> 00:33:58.486
A second tier sums across nodes, and results are broadcast.

396
00:33:58.766 --> 00:34:04.675
Counts can be approximate, but the aggregation must never create hot keys (Ch 51).

397
00:34:04.955 --> 00:34:20.422
Telemetry QoE: streaming aggregations per (CDN, ISP, region, device) per minute, using approximate distinct counts (HyperLogLog) for viewers and percentile sketches (t-digest or DDSketch) for start times.

398
00:34:21.772 --> 00:34:23.372
Ad decisioning and pacing.

399
00:34:23.652 --> 00:34:34.084
Selection: eligible campaigns filtered by targeting and frequency caps, ranked by price times predicted completion or relevance, with competitive separation.

400
00:34:34.364 --> 00:34:39.015
Pacing: throttle each campaign so its budget is spent evenly across the match.

401
00:34:39.295 --> 00:34:45.481
A PID-style controller adjusts participation probability (Ch 49 L3).

402
00:34:45.761 --> 00:34:53.185
Forecasting: inventory (impressions per break) estimated from the concurrency forecast, to sell guaranteed campaigns.

403
00:34:54.535 --> 00:34:55.815
Anomaly detection.

404
00:34:56.095 --> 00:35:03.690
QoE anomalies: rebuffer spikes per ISP or CDN, detected by seasonal baselines and control charts.

405
00:35:03.970 --> 00:35:09.666
Fraud: credential stuffing, account sharing beyond plan rules, ad fraud (invalid traffic).

406
00:35:11.016 --> 00:35:11.955
Part 7.

407
00:35:12.235 --> 00:35:13.557
Data Architecture.

408
00:35:14.807 --> 00:35:16.194
Operational stores.

409
00:35:16.474 --> 00:35:17.818
Here is a quick table:

410
00:35:18.098 --> 00:35:20.274
Data, and Store (reference), and Notes.

411
00:35:20.554 --> 00:35:22.666
Accounts, profiles, subscriptions.

412
00:35:22.946 --> 00:35:24.183
Store (reference):

413
00:35:24.463 --> 00:35:27.962
Relational (PostgreSQL or MySQL, sharded).

414
00:35:28.242 --> 00:35:29.053
Notes:

415
00:35:29.333 --> 00:35:30.826
Transactions for billing.

416
00:35:31.106 --> 00:35:32.791
Entitlements (read path).

417
00:35:33.071 --> 00:35:34.309
Store (reference):

418
00:35:34.589 --> 00:35:38.407
Key-value cache (Redis or EVCache) replicated to edges.

419
00:35:38.687 --> 00:35:39.498
Notes:

420
00:35:39.778 --> 00:35:41.655
Read-heavy, latency-critical.

421
00:35:41.935 --> 00:35:43.855
Viewing history, resume points.

422
00:35:44.135 --> 00:35:45.373
Store (reference):

423
00:35:45.653 --> 00:35:47.359
Wide-column (Cassandra).

424
00:35:47.639 --> 00:35:48.450
Notes:

425
00:35:48.730 --> 00:35:51.290
High write volume, per-profile partitions.

426
00:35:51.570 --> 00:35:53.042
Catalogue and metadata.

427
00:35:53.322 --> 00:35:54.559
Store (reference):

428
00:35:54.839 --> 00:35:56.781
Document store + search index.

429
00:35:57.061 --> 00:35:57.871
Notes:

430
00:35:58.151 --> 00:36:00.114
Versioned; events to downstream.

431
00:36:00.394 --> 00:36:01.823
Live session counters.

432
00:36:02.103 --> 00:36:03.341
Store (reference):

433
00:36:03.621 --> 00:36:05.285
Sharded in-memory counters.

434
00:36:05.565 --> 00:36:06.375
Notes:

435
00:36:06.655 --> 00:36:09.279
Concurrent stream limits, concurrency metrics.

436
00:36:10.629 --> 00:36:11.760
Media assets.

