623
00:45:43.015 --> 00:45:43.953
Part 12.

624
00:45:44.233 --> 00:45:45.577
Interview Q and A.

625
00:45:46.827 --> 00:45:47.659
Beginner.

626
00:45:47.939 --> 00:45:48.963
Question 1.

627
00:45:49.243 --> 00:45:51.995
What happens between pressing Play and seeing video?

628
00:45:52.275 --> 00:45:53.641
Thirty-second answer:

629
00:45:53.921 --> 00:46:13.270
The app asks the playback service, which checks entitlement, rights and device; returns a device-specific manifest, signed CDN URLs and a DRM licence token; the player gets a licence, downloads the first segments at a safe bitrate, starts playback, and adapts bitrate while sending telemetry.

630
00:46:14.620 --> 00:46:15.623
Question 2.

631
00:46:15.903 --> 00:46:17.780
What is adaptive bitrate streaming?

632
00:46:18.060 --> 00:46:19.425
Thirty-second answer:

633
00:46:19.705 --> 00:46:30.884
Video is encoded at several quality levels and cut into short segments; the player picks each segment's quality based on measured bandwidth and buffer, so it can switch down instead of stalling.

634
00:46:32.234 --> 00:46:33.279
Question 3.

635
00:46:33.559 --> 00:46:36.055
Why do streaming services use CDNs?

636
00:46:36.335 --> 00:46:37.701
Thirty-second answer:

637
00:46:37.981 --> 00:46:50.973
Video is huge; serving it from one place would overload the origin and add latency; CDNs cache segments near viewers, and ISP-embedded caches keep traffic inside the viewer's network.

638
00:46:52.323 --> 00:46:53.389
Question 4.

639
00:46:53.669 --> 00:46:56.485
How do live streams differ from VOD technically?

640
00:46:56.765 --> 00:46:58.131
Thirty-second answer:

641
00:46:58.411 --> 00:47:14.325
Live is encoded and packaged in real time with a seconds-level delay budget, manifests change every segment, everyone watches the same moment (so caching must coalesce requests), and traffic arrives in sudden spikes rather than a smooth curve.

642
00:47:15.675 --> 00:47:16.678
Intermediate.

643
00:47:16.958 --> 00:47:18.067
Question 5.

644
00:47:18.347 --> 00:47:20.801
How do you build the encoding ladder for a title?

645
00:47:21.081 --> 00:47:22.446
Thirty-second answer:

646
00:47:22.726 --> 00:47:37.702
Encode sample points across resolutions and bitrates, score them with a perceptual metric like VMAF, take the convex hull of quality versus bitrate, and pick rungs along it, per title or per shot, so simple content uses fewer bits.

647
00:47:39.052 --> 00:47:40.161
Question 6.

648
00:47:40.441 --> 00:47:43.151
How do you protect premium content from piracy?

649
00:47:43.431 --> 00:47:44.796
Thirty-second answer:

650
00:47:45.076 --> 00:47:57.428
DRM encryption with device-appropriate licence servers and policies, short-lived token-authenticated URLs, forensic watermarking per session for live sport, and monitoring with takedowns.

651
00:47:58.778 --> 00:47:59.866
Question 7.

652
00:48:00.146 --> 00:48:02.386
How are ads inserted into a live match?

653
00:48:02.666 --> 00:48:04.031
Thirty-second answer:

654
00:48:04.311 --> 00:48:21.250
SCTE-35 markers signal breaks; at each break the SSAI service asks ad decisioning for each viewer cohort's ad pod and stitches pre-transcoded ad segments into the manifest; the player plays them seamlessly; impressions are beaconed and reconciled.

655
00:48:22.600 --> 00:48:23.624
Question 8.

656
00:48:23.904 --> 00:48:27.104
How are subscriptions and entitlements enforced at playback?

657
00:48:27.384 --> 00:48:28.749
Thirty-second answer:

658
00:48:29.029 --> 00:48:43.685
Payments create or renew subscriptions; the entitlements service maps plans to rights (quality, devices, ad-free, sports); the playback service checks cached entitlements, rights windows and concurrent-stream counters on every play.

659
00:48:45.035 --> 00:48:46.102
Question 9.

660
00:48:46.382 --> 00:48:49.923
How would you show live scores and reactions to 40M viewers?

661
00:48:50.203 --> 00:48:51.569
Thirty-second answer:

662
00:48:51.849 --> 00:49:07.657
Scores from the official feed published as small CDN-cached JSON with short TTL (or pushed over persistent connections); reactions batched on clients, aggregated per second at ingestion tiers, and only aggregated counts broadcast.

663
00:49:09.007 --> 00:49:09.775
Senior.

664
00:49:10.055 --> 00:49:11.100
Question 10.

665
00:49:11.380 --> 00:49:15.412
How would you handle 40 million concurrent viewers for a cricket final?

666
00:49:15.692 --> 00:49:17.057
Thirty-second answer:

667
00:49:17.337 --> 00:49:30.457
Data plane: redundant contribution and encoding, CMAF packaging, origin shield with request coalescing, multi-CDN plus ISP caches with reserved capacity, bitrate caps under stress.

668
00:49:30.737 --> 00:49:44.028
Control plane: forecast joins, pre-scale, static cacheable pages, edge token validation, degradation switches, client backoff with jitter, and a war room steering CDNs by real-time QoE.

669
00:49:45.378 --> 00:49:46.509
Question 11.

670
00:49:46.789 --> 00:49:48.581
Compare ABR algorithms.

671
00:49:48.861 --> 00:49:50.226
Thirty-second answer:

672
00:49:50.506 --> 00:50:11.306
Throughput-based reacts to bandwidth but oscillates; buffer-based is stable but slow to start; BOLA optimises utility with buffer; MPC plans a few segments ahead with predicted throughput; learned policies adapt but are harder to explain, production players blend throughput at start with buffer-based control and tune by A or B tests.

673
00:50:12.656 --> 00:50:13.744
Question 12.

674
00:50:14.024 --> 00:50:15.667
How does CDN steering work?

675
00:50:15.947 --> 00:50:17.312
Thirty-second answer:

676
00:50:17.592 --> 00:50:34.744
Aggregate per-minute QoE per CDN, ISP and region from player telemetry; assign new sessions to the best CDN subject to capacity and cost commitments, keep a small exploration share per CDN (bandit), and let players switch on errors mid-session.

677
00:50:36.094 --> 00:50:37.374
Question 13.

678
00:50:37.654 --> 00:50:40.150
How do you make the live pipeline fault-tolerant?

679
00:50:40.430 --> 00:50:41.795
Thirty-second answer:

680
00:50:42.075 --> 00:50:55.985
1 plus1 at every hop, two contribution paths, two regions, time-aligned paired encoders, redundant packagers and origins, multiple CDNs, with automatic switchover and rehearsed failovers before the season.

681
00:50:57.335 --> 00:50:58.529
Question 14.

682
00:50:58.809 --> 00:51:01.369
How do you design the recommendation home page?

683
00:51:01.649 --> 00:51:03.015
Thirty-second answer:

684
00:51:03.295 --> 00:51:22.239
Candidate sources (collaborative filtering, content similarity, trending, continue watching), a ranking model predicting play-and-keep-watching, page construction with row selection and diversity, personalised artwork via contextual bandits, and A or B testing on retention and hours.

685
00:51:23.589 --> 00:51:24.485
Architect.

686
00:51:24.765 --> 00:51:25.959
Question 15.

687
00:51:26.239 --> 00:51:29.439
Design the telemetry and QoE analytics platform.

688
00:51:29.719 --> 00:51:31.085
Thirty-second answer:

689
00:51:31.365 --> 00:51:52.485
Players batch events to edge collectors, then Kafka, then Flink computing per-minute QoE aggregates with sketches (HLL, t-digest) into a real-time OLAP store for dashboards and CDN steering; raw events into an Iceberg lakehouse for analysis, ML and experiments; sampling and retention tiers to control cost.

690
00:51:53.835 --> 00:51:55.029
Question 16.

691
00:51:55.309 --> 00:51:59.064
Build your own CDN appliances or use commercial CDNs?

692
00:51:59.344 --> 00:52:00.709
Thirty-second answer:

693
00:52:00.989 --> 00:52:16.541
At very large VOD scale, ISP-embedded caches filled off-peak cut cost and improve quality; commercial CDNs add burst capacity and coverage, especially for live; most large services combine both with a steering layer.

694
00:52:17.891 --> 00:52:19.235
Question 17.

695
00:52:19.515 --> 00:52:22.758
How would you forecast and prepare capacity for a tournament?

696
00:52:23.038 --> 00:52:24.403
Thirty-second answer:

697
00:52:24.683 --> 00:52:42.198
Model peak concurrency and join curves per match from teams, stage, slot and history; reserve CDN capacity and pre-scale control planes per match; load test the join curve; update forecasts in-match; align ad inventory and ops staffing to the forecast.

698
00:52:43.548 --> 00:52:44.721
Question 18.

699
00:52:45.001 --> 00:52:47.433
What is the economics of encoding choices?

700
00:52:47.713 --> 00:52:49.079
Thirty-second answer:

701
00:52:49.359 --> 00:53:05.359
Better codecs and per-title optimisation cost compute once but save bandwidth on every view; prioritise by expected views and device support, re-encode the back catalogue by expected savings, and measure quality with VMAF to avoid regressions.

702
00:53:06.709 --> 00:53:07.946
Question 19.

703
00:53:08.226 --> 00:53:11.341
How do you use chaos engineering for a streaming platform?

704
00:53:11.621 --> 00:53:12.986
Thirty-second answer:

705
00:53:13.266 --> 00:53:29.138
Randomly terminate instances and inject dependency failures in production under controls, rehearse regional evacuation, verify degradation switches and fallbacks, and run game days before major events, with abort criteria and observability.

706
00:53:30.488 --> 00:53:31.597
Question 20.

707
00:53:31.877 --> 00:53:36.933
What are the main differences between designing a VOD service and a live sports service?

708
00:53:37.213 --> 00:53:38.579
Thirty-second answer:

709
00:53:38.859 --> 00:53:59.125
VOD optimises bits per quality and caches pre-positioned popular content with smooth demand; live optimises real-time redundancy, latency, request coalescing and survives synchronised join spikes, so live's hardest problems are the control plane and delivery capacity, not encoding efficiency.

710
00:54:00.475 --> 00:54:01.521
Part 13.

711
00:54:01.801 --> 00:54:02.569
Summary.

712
00:54:03.819 --> 00:54:15.125
Two planes: a simple, redundant, cacheable data plane for video, and a control plane for rights, entitlements, ads and steering that must survive flash-sale-like join spikes.

713
00:54:15.405 --> 00:54:24.771
VOD wins on encoding efficiency (per-title and shot-based ladders, VMAF, modern codecs) and on pre-positioning in ISP caches.

714
00:54:25.051 --> 00:54:35.483
Live wins on redundancy (1 plus1 everywhere), request coalescing, multi-CDN steering by real-time QoE, pre-scaling and degradation switches.

715
00:54:36.833 --> 00:54:48.097
Algorithms run everything: convex-hull ladders, ABR control, bandit-based CDN and artwork choice, popularity-aware caching, concurrency forecasting, ad pacing.

716
00:54:48.377 --> 00:54:58.830
Telemetry is the nervous system: millions of events per second, turned into per-minute QoE for steering, and into the lakehouse for product, content and ML decisions.

